<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-16"?>
<!--
  Windows Task Scheduler definition for the nightly encrypted database backup.

  THIS IS A TEMPLATE. IT HAS NOT BEEN IMPORTED ANYWHERE, AND IT MUST NOT BE
  IMPORTED ON THE PRODUCTION HOST YET. Installing it starts taking production
  backups, which requires the off-site destination, the key custody
  arrangement and the retention decision to exist first — see
  docs/operations/BACKUP_RUNBOOK.md section 8.

  Before importing, replace every {{PLACEHOLDER}} and read the notes at the
  bottom of this file. Import with:

      schtasks /Create /TN "DropPilot Nightly Backup" /XML droppilot-backup.xml /RU {{SERVICE_ACCOUNT}}

  schtasks prompts for the account's password; it is never written here.
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>
      Takes one encrypted, verifiable backup of the DropPilot production
      database, then verifies it. Exit codes are meaningful: 0 succeeded,
      2 configuration refused, 3 pg_dump failed, 4 encryption failed,
      5 verification failed, 6 the filesystem refused. Any non-zero result is
      an operator page the same day.
    </Description>
    <URI>\DropPilot\Nightly Backup</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>

  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <!--
        A dedicated service account. It must be:
          * a member of no administrative group;
          * granted "Log on as a batch job" (secpol.msc → Local Policies →
            User Rights Assignment);
          * denied interactive and network logon;
          * given write access to BACKUP_DIRECTORY and read access to the
            checkout, and nothing else;
          * backed by a PostgreSQL role with CONNECT and SELECT only — not the
            application's role, which can write.
        Running this as the interactive operator account gives a nightly
        unattended process the operator's full authority, which is the
        privilege the backup exists to survive losing.
      -->
      <UserId>{{SERVICE_ACCOUNT}}</UserId>
      <LogonType>Password</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>

  <Settings>
    <!-- One instance, ever. A dump that overruns must not have a second one
         start on top of it: two pg_dumps against one database is load the
         database did not plan for, and two writers in one directory is a
         race over the .part names. -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>

    <!-- A dump that has not finished in four hours has gone wrong. Killing it
         is safe: nothing is published until it exits zero. -->
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT4H</ExecutionTimeLimit>

    <!-- Run late rather than not at all if the machine was off at 02:30. A
         missed window then shows up as marker AGE in the readiness check,
         which is the real detector — see the runbook. -->
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>

    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>

    <!-- Retry twice, twenty minutes apart. A transient lock or a busy disk is
         worth retrying; a configuration error is not, and exits 2 immediately
         on each attempt so the retries cost nothing and the last result is
         still 2. -->
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT20M</Interval>
      <Count>2</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>

  <Triggers>
    <CalendarTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2026-01-01T02:30:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <ScheduleByDay>
        <DaysInterval>1</DaysInterval>
      </ScheduleByDay>
    </CalendarTrigger>
  </Triggers>

  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>{{BACKEND_DIR}}\.venv\Scripts\python.exe</Command>
      <!--
        No secret appears here, and none may ever be added. Credentials come
        from the .env that WorkingDirectory selects, which is ACL'd separately.
        A command line is visible to every user on the machine.
      -->
      <Arguments>scripts\create_database_backup.py --expect-database {{DATABASE}} --allow-production</Arguments>
      <!--
        Not tidiness. The tools read the .env beside the checkout, so a wrong
        working directory backs up the wrong database with the wrong key.
      -->
      <WorkingDirectory>{{BACKEND_DIR}}</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>

<!--
  NOTES — read before importing.

  1. VERIFY AS A SEPARATE TASK. This task takes the backup; a second task
     should run

         scripts\verify_database_backup.py --all

     an hour later. Keeping them separate means a verification failure is
     distinguishable from a dump failure in the task history, and a slow
     verify never delays the next dump.

  2. PRUNE AS A THIRD TASK, AND ONLY AFTER A REHEARSAL HAS BEEN READ. Run

         scripts\prune_database_backups.py

     with no flags first — it rehearses and changes nothing. Add --apply only
     once an operator has read a report and agreed with it.

  3. ALERTING IS NOT INCLUDED AND DOES NOT EXIST. Task Scheduler has none
     built in. Two signals must be monitored:
       (a) this task's Last Run Result, non-zero;
       (b) scripts\verify_production_config.py reporting BACKUPS as anything
           other than PASS — which covers the case this task never ran at all,
           because a task that does not run produces no failing result.
     Signal (b) is the important one. (a) cannot detect silence.

  4. THE SERVICE ACCOUNT'S PASSWORD EXPIRING stops the task with a task-level
     error, not a backup error. Set it not to expire, or monitor for it. This
     is the most common way a Windows backup schedule dies quietly.

  5. LOGS. Redirect stdout and stderr into the backup directory, which is
     ACL'd to the service account:

         ...python.exe scripts\create_database_backup.py ... >> {{BACKUP_DIR}}\backup.log 2>&1

     Doing that needs cmd.exe as the Command and the whole line as Arguments,
     which puts the redirection on a command line — harmless, since no secret
     is on it. Task Scheduler's own history records the exit code either way.

  6. OFF-SITE COPYING IS NOT PART OF THIS TASK and must not be bolted on
     without review. A copy step with delete permission on the remote is the
     mechanism by which ransomware on this host destroys the archive it was
     supposed to be safe from. The remote credential must not be able to
     delete.
-->
